course: dictionary
lesson: 7819
title: Quietly: more than no noise
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: «Quietly» is an adverb. It tells you how someone does something: how they speak, walk, wait or work. The main meaning is making little or no noise, as in «Close the door quietly.»
explain: It can also mean calmly and without fuss. «She quietly helped» means she helped without making a big show of it. Read the words around it to pick the right meaning: «He spoke quietly» means in a gentle, low voice, and «The school helped quietly» means without any fuss.
fact: «Quietly» is an adverb, so it tells how something is done.
fact: The forms are quietly, more quietly and most quietly.
fact: «Quietly» means making little or no noise, calmly without fuss, or in a gentle, low voice.
fact: In stories, «quietly» often shows someone sneaking, or staying calm.
fact: A good check is to swap in another meaning, such as "without noise" or "calmly", and see if the sentence still makes sense.
exercise: «Close the door quietly.» What does quietly mean here? | *Making little or no noise | Angrily | With a big smile | Very fast
exercise: «She quietly helped her friend.» What does quietly mean here? | *Calmly, without fuss | Sadly | Slowly and painfully | Loudly, so everyone could hear
exercise: Mia waits quietly until everyone is served. What does quietly tell us? | What Mia is waiting for | Where Mia is sitting | Who Mia is sitting with | *How Mia waits: calmly and without making a noise
test: Ella speaks quietly in the library. What does quietly mean? | Not at all, because she is silent | Very loudly, so the librarian can hear | *In a gentle, low voice | Angrily and fast
test: Which sentence uses quietly to mean calmly, without fuss? | The bell rang quietly at noon | *The nurse quietly sorted out the problem before anyone noticed | The thunder was quietly loud | The alarm rang loudly all night
exam: «The school helped quietly.» What does quietly mean here? | *Calmly, without making a fuss | Angrily, after a fight | Late in the afternoon | Very loudly, with a band playing
